In article <a4e7ed0e.0304020742.ed80661@posting.google.com>,
	djsimmers@netscape.net (web_djs) writes:
> Ok, TRU64 newbie here.  I am finding that after my profile runs my
> path is full of crap I don't want/need.  Since I can't figure out
> where it's coming from, how do I clear my path?

It may depend on your shell.  There is a file called .login most likely
in your home directory or /etc that will have your path set.  It will
look like this:
PATH=$HOME/bin:/sbin:/usr/sbin:/usr/local/bin:/usr/X11/bin:.
export PATH

This is the path for a bourne type shell, sh, bash, ksh.  You will see
something like this for csh in a file called .cshrc:

set path = (. /sbin /usr/sbin /usr/bin /usr/bin/X11 )

You can edit the file apropriate for your shell to change it.  You may
also have .bash_profile or another file that may have a path set in it.
You can search for PATH or path in any .filename file.

HTH,

Sam


