Andrew Duane USG wrote:
> In article <779p8v858l5t5mmssv2gfviqb3rd1v5sbo@4ax.com> dgoolsby@jam.rr.com writes:
> 
>>has anyone ever heard or seen a recommended reboot time for tru64
>>machines?  just curious.. i know Winders products need at least
>>monthly reboots, just did not know if tru64 had a recommended one.
> 
> 
> Not needed.
>

And not needed more most modern UNIX-like operating systems. There *are* 
some potential advantages to very occasionally powering down a system 
and rebooting it at a controlled time, especially if you do a good pair 
of backups first. It's a good way to test the behavior of the system in 
case of a power outage or other forced shutdown, and a good way to find 
bad disks. It also gives you a chance to fsck your root partition, or do 
any re-partitioning of normally active services. As good as modern SCSI 
RAID arrays are, drives do wear out eventually and sometimes benefit 
from a chance to restart the thing and "look under the hood".

But it's certainly not needed for the memory leakage and disk 
fragmentations that was so typical of older Microsoft releases, both 
operating systems and software.



