The easiest way to prevent all logins to the system for a period of
time is to just create the file /etc/nologin.  Nobody can login while
this file exists.

>
> I scanned through the man page for dtlogin since we use CDE once users
> login.  It mentions right down the bottom that one of the possible
> messages it can spit out is "Logins are currently disabled" which gives
> me hope that I can prevent people logging on completely but I couldn't
> work out how to actually activate this.

I don't know much about CDE, but it was my impression that XDM and
CDE are two different window managers.  At any rate, I took a quick
look at the CDE Advanced User's and System Administrator's guide.
http://h30097.www3.hp.com/docs/base_doc/DOCUMENTATION/V40G_HTML/SYSADMIN/TIT
LE.HTM
There is a section in chapter 1 titled "Controlling Access to the Login
Server"
that might do what you want at that level.

>
> > What version of Tru64 UNIX are you running?
>
> I'm running 4.0F with no enhanced security.
>

Neither of the above will affect any users that are currently logged in,
if you need to make sure that nobody is logged in while the maintenance
is performed the easiest way would probably be to just bring the
system down to single user mode and bring it back up when you're
done.




