Liu wrote:

> Hello.
> I'm a newbie to RAID. Doing something on RAID , I found it somewhat slow.
> so I did a simple test. just copy a big file(sized almost exactly 2G byte)
> to itself. (date;cp bigfile ..;date)
> 
> it took 4min 4sec. I copied on itself so, estimated speed is 17M byte/sec.
> 
> I'm using compaq RA3000 & RA8000 (each on diffrent machine of cource).
> but the result is almost same. Each RAID is mirrored and striped. No
> parity checking. Accoriding to spec., RA3000 uses UltraSCSI2 & has max
> bandwidth of 39.5 Mbyte/sec.
> 
> Is it normal transfer rate of 17Mb/sec for RAID with above configuration??
> 
> (I found suspicious thing though, all disks in raid has a 10K RPM in spec.
> but by using 'disklabel -p' they say that they have 3.6K RPM.)
> 
> Thanx for ur kind answer in advance.

If you copied the data file to itself, then you need to remember that you 
read the disk, and wrote the disk at the same time.  So, if you're seeing a 
speed of 17MB/sec, then remember that it was 17MB/sec reading, then 
17MB/sec writing, which is fairly close to the 39.5MB/sec THEORETICAL 
maximum transfer rate.

Also, the disks behind the RA[38]000 controller are important.  

While the disks themselves are 10K RPM, the unit presented by the RA[38]000 
controller do not reflect the disks themselves.  There used to be a time 
when device drivers cared about the rotational speed of disks, but not any 
more, especially with RAID controllers.  So, 3.6K RPM is a nice "default" 
value.
-- 

        Stu


