Just use iozone or bonnie, they will give you the numbers most relevant
to your real world experience (well, maybe).  They ask the OS to sync
the data as soon as it is written, but of course the large cache on the
RAID controller will mask some of that.  You must then try to use file
data even larger than the cache (multiple gigabytes).

Even "raw devices" would still be cached at the controller, unless the
OS/device driver/RAID controller had some way of indicating direct writes
to drives (I have no idea if this is the case).

Note that, it's worthwhile reading the specs of the RAID controller and
the drive array and being knowledgeable about its structure (you said
you looked at the array configuration and it was mirrored and striped
on one controller; whether that causes you to see a performance hit
at the FS level depends on a lot of factors).  We were wondering about
the lower read and write performance of our RAID array until we looked
more closely at the specs.  Although it has a fibre channel interface,
the controller has a maximum throughput of 16MB/s a second.

Dave


